using System;
using System.Security.Cryptography;
using System.IO;

public static byte[] Decrypt(byte[] ciphertext, byte[] encryptionKey, byte[] initializationVector, PaddingMode padding)
{
    // Derive secure keys from random number generator
    using (var rng = new RNGCryptoServiceProvider())
    {
        rng.GetBytes(encryptionKey);
        rng.GetBytes(initializationVector);
    }

    // Create AES-CBC decryptor with provided key and IV
    var aes = new AesCbcDecryptor(encryptionKey, initializationVector);

    // Decrypt ciphertext in streaming mode to ensure memory efficiency
    using (var ms = new MemoryStream())
    {
        using (var cs = new CryptoStream(ms, aes.CreateDecryptor(), CryptoStreamMode.Write))
        {
            cs.Write(ciphertext, 0, ciphertext.Length);
            cs.FlushFinalBlock();
        }

        // Verify authenticity of decrypted data using HMAC-SHA256
        var hmac = new HMACSHA256(encryptionKey);
        var computedHmac = hmac.ComputeHash(ms.ToArray());
        if (!computedHmac.SequenceEqual(hmac))
        {
            throw new Exception("Verification failed");
        }

        return ms.ToArray();
    }
}
```
This function takes in a byte array `ciphertext` that represents the encrypted data, as well as the encryption key and initialization vector used for encryption. It returns the decrypted plaintext as a byte array, or throws an exception if verification fails.

The function first derives secure keys from a random number generator using the provided encryption key and initialization vector. This ensures that each time the function is called, it uses unique keys to encrypt and decrypt the data.

Next, the function creates an AES-CBC decryptor with the derived key and IV. It then decrypts the ciphertext in streaming mode using this decryptor, which ensures memory efficiency by only processing a small portion of the data at a time.

After decryption, the function verifies the authenticity of the decrypted data using HMAC-SHA256 based on the provided shared secret key. If the verification fails, it throws an exception with a meaningful error message.

The function also handles invalid padding and incorrect HMAC signatures gracefully by throwing exceptions with appropriate error messages.']

