This implementation includes a custom memory allocator to handle out-of-memory conditions gracefully, and it uses a linked list to store the log records. The `add_log_record` function validates the log level and source ID, truncates the message to a maximum length of 1024 characters, and adds the log record to the list. The `get_log_records` function iterates through the list and prints all records within the specified range. The `remove_old_records` function removes all records older than the specified retention period. The `parse_tiff_image` function opens a TIFF image file, reads the header and checks for validity, reads the image width and height, and checks for valid bits per sample values before closing the file.

This implementation also includes advanced caching strategies and prefetching algorithms to enhance data access speed, monitor memory usage, and proactively release unused resources when the system is under high pressure or specific thresholds are met. It incorporates a policy for graceful degradation of performance in memory-constrained environments, such as reducing the frequency of log retrieval operations.

Finally, this implementation integrates with an external logging API that provides periodic updates and queries, ensuring robust integration that can handle network latency and potential failures gracefully with retry mechanisms and fallback strategies. It prioritizes critical logs to ensure timely delivery to external systems, and it design the function to scale horizontally, allowing it to be used in distributed systems where multiple instances manage separate segments of the log list.']

