The function first checks if the TIFF file can be opened successfully, and if not, prints an error message to stderr and returns an empty struct. If the file is opened successfully, it extracts the image width and height using `TIFFGetField`, and then uses these values to calculate the number of pixels in the image (`npixels`). It then extracts the bits per sample and samples per pixel using `TIFFGetField`, and checks if the number of pixels in the image is consistent with the product of the number of samples per pixel and the number of pixels. If there are discrepancies, it prints a warning message to stderr.

The function then extracts the compression method and resolution using `TIFFGetField`, and stores them in the struct. It also extracts the color space and extra samples using `TIFFGetField`, and stores them in the struct as well.

Finally, the function closes the TIFF file using `TIFFClose` and returns the extracted information in the form of a struct. If there is an unsupported compression method or color space, the function prints an error message indicating the specific issue and returns an empty struct.']

